import{_ as i,o as t,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.CqpfiAoK.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Entry 257 | Tang-Luo Decomposition | Critique of Pseudo-Universal-Benefit: The Limit of Good Will and the Absence of Institutions","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Entry 257 | Tang-Luo Decomposition | Critique of Pseudo-Universal-Benefit: The Limit of Good Will and the Absence of Institutions","no":257,"module":2,"tags":["Tang-Luo","Pseudo-Universal-Benefit","Good-Will Limit","Institutional Absence","Myriad-Realms Benchmark"],"related":["03-Symbiotic Civilization Charter","40-Symbiotic Constitution General Outline","01-Anchor Symbiotic Civilization Manifesto"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/0257_模块2-唐罗-伪普惠批判篇.md","filePath":"en/0257_模块2-唐罗-伪普惠批判篇.md"}'),a={name:"en/0257_模块2-唐罗-伪普惠批判篇.md"};function s(r,e,l,u,d,h){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="entry-257-tang-luo-decomposition-critique-of-pseudo-universal-benefit-the-limit-of-good-will-and-the-absence-of-institutions" tabindex="-1">Entry 257 | Tang-Luo Decomposition | Critique of Pseudo-Universal-Benefit: The Limit of Good Will and the Absence of Institutions <a class="header-anchor" href="#entry-257-tang-luo-decomposition-critique-of-pseudo-universal-benefit-the-limit-of-good-will-and-the-absence-of-institutions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Entry 257 | Tang-Luo Decomposition | Critique of Pseudo-Universal-Benefit: The Limit of Good Will and the Absence of Institutions&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Module: Module 2 · Myriad-Realms Benchmark Critique Library | Series: Full Decomposition of the Tang-Luo System (Entry 7)</p></blockquote><h2 id="introduction-why-a-good-person-s-good-deeds-do-not-count" tabindex="-1">Introduction: Why a good person&#39;s good deeds do not count <a class="header-anchor" href="#introduction-why-a-good-person-s-good-deeds-do-not-count" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Introduction: Why a good person&#39;s good deeds do not count&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The order of the six top-level axioms is: the anti-predation axiom, the republican axiom of Myriad Races, the collective ascension axiom, the causal self-discipline axiom, the law-first axiom, and the universal equal infinite law. The universal equal infinite law: within the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization, all beings inherently possess basic infinite power; the easygoing retain a guaranteed basic infinity and will never be eliminated; strivers and Youth Supremes can superimpose more and higher levels of infinity. Infinite origin is inherent in all, so predation naturally loses its meaning. Note the last one — the law-first axiom ranks at the highest execution position of the axiom system. Tang Luo&#39;s problem is not that he is not beneficent, but that his &quot;beneficence&quot; is not written into law. This entry takes the law-first axiom as core: universal benefit not written into law is only good will; good will is laudable, but institutions are absent. Protagonist uniqueness dependency judgment: <strong>Tang Luo = high dependency / bestowal-style civilization</strong>.</p><h2 id="i-energy-level-waste-analysis-of-this-system-s-strong-ones" tabindex="-1">I. &quot;Energy-Level Waste&quot; Analysis of This System&#39;s Strong Ones <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-energy-level-waste-analysis-of-this-system-s-strong-ones" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I. &quot;Energy-Level Waste&quot; Analysis of This System&#39;s Strong Ones&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Pseudo-universal-benefit&#39;s energy-level waste is &quot;maintainability&quot; waste:</p><ul><li><strong>No-backup good will</strong>: each of Tang Luo&#39;s good policies has no backup, no successor, no operations manual. Runs well when he is alive; once he retires, all good policies become &quot;legacy favors&quot; — legacy favors are naturally weathered by time</li><li><strong>No quantified standards</strong>: how much universal benefit is enough? Who benefits, how much, how to monitor? No data at all. Tang Luo almsgives by feel, civilization benefits by luck</li><li><strong>No correction mechanism</strong>: when universal-benefit policy deviates, there is no Alaya Network feedback, no Symbiotic Court adjudication, no supervision committee questioning. If wrong, can only wait for Tang Luo himself to discover it</li></ul><p>Energy-level waste&#39;s essence: good policies built at civilization-reconstruction-tier cost have, due to lack of institutionalization, <strong>extremely short half-life</strong>. Good policies are not overthrown, they are forgotten.</p><h2 id="ii-why-this-civilization-inevits-historical-regression" tabindex="-1">II. Why This Civilization Inevits Historical Regression <a class="header-anchor" href="#ii-why-this-civilization-inevits-historical-regression" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;II. Why This Civilization Inevits Historical Regression&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Legacy-favor decay law</strong>: every generation, the understanding of &quot;how Tang Luo thought back then&quot; decays one layer. After three generations, good policies only have names left, the spirit is dead</li><li><strong>Inertial restoration</strong>: old aristocratic families&#39; remnants, new powerholders&#39; ambitions, all wait for the day Tang Luo is gone. They need not positively overthrow universal benefit, only slowly hollow it out — change school rules, change quotas, change curricula</li><li><strong>Legitimacy transfer</strong>: when Tang Luo is in office, legitimacy comes from him personally; once he dies, a legitimacy vacuum appears. Who fills it? There have always been only two answers: a new strongman, or restoration of old institutions. Tang Luo left no third answer — a constitution</li></ol><p>Regression form: within one or two generations after Tang Luo&#39;s death, civilization slowly slides back onto the pre-reform track. Not dramatic collapse, but slow-boiled frog restoration.</p><h2 id="iii-deep-decomposition-of-the-protagonist-s-predatory-thinking" tabindex="-1">III. Deep Decomposition of the Protagonist&#39;s Predatory Thinking <a class="header-anchor" href="#iii-deep-decomposition-of-the-protagonist-s-predatory-thinking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;III. Deep Decomposition of the Protagonist&#39;s Predatory Thinking&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Not legislating is predation</strong>: Tang Luo gave all beings benefits, but did not write these benefits into &quot;all beings&#39; inalienable rights.&quot; Benefits not written into law can be withdrawn at any time — this is equivalent to disguisedly telling all beings: these were never yours in the first place</li><li><strong>Good-will substituting institutions is predation</strong>: replacing &quot;institutions guarantee you well&quot; with &quot;I am good to you&quot; deprives all beings of the ability to hold their own fate in hand</li><li><strong>Rule of man substituting rule of law is predation</strong>: civilization&#39;s operation rests on one person&#39;s virtue, and virtue is not transmissible. Taking the non-transmissible as the foundation of state is itself irresponsible to civilization</li></ul><p>By the Gu Chensha / Tang Luo judgment table standard: Tang Luo is the <strong>enlightened predator</strong> — &quot;enlightened&quot; reaches its maximum in this entry, and &quot;predation&quot; appears in its most hidden form. He is not a bad person, he is a good person who has not yet learned to write a constitution. The &quot;righteous rogue&quot; he lacks now concretizes as: does he dare rewrite his own good from &quot;I am willing&quot; to &quot;you must have.&quot;</p><h2 id="iv-comparison-with-anchor-symbiosis-s-complete-alternative" tabindex="-1">IV. Comparison With Anchor Symbiosis&#39;s Complete Alternative <a class="header-anchor" href="#iv-comparison-with-anchor-symbiosis-s-complete-alternative" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IV. Comparison With Anchor Symbiosis&#39;s Complete Alternative&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tang-Luo Pseudo-Universal-Benefit</th><th>Anchor Symbiosis&#39;s True Universal Benefit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Good policies maintained by Tang Luo</td><td>Myriad Races Republican Civilization Charter written into constitution, good policies are statutes not legacy favors</td></tr><tr><td>No monitoring no feedback</td><td>Alaya Network + Anchor Supervision Committee, universal-benefit effects quantified in real time</td></tr><tr><td>Rule of man</td><td>Anchor Council legislates, Symbiotic Executive Committee administers, Symbiotic Court adjudicates</td></tr><tr><td>Can be withdrawn</td><td>Anti-monopoly law, Everyone-Has-a-Road Guarantee Law, Education Guarantee Law backstop; withdrawal is unconstitutional</td></tr><tr><td>Legacy-favor decay</td><td>Statutory indexing maintained, every good policy has a number, case law, interpretation</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Alternative&#39;s crux: Tang Luo did everything right, except he missed the step of &quot;writing it down.&quot; Heng Yuan Zhou legislates this step — good policies not in the constitution equal not done.</p><h2 id="v-quest-sediment-domain-simulation-review-plan" tabindex="-1">V. Quest Sediment Domain Simulation Review Plan <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-quest-sediment-domain-simulation-review-plan" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V. Quest Sediment Domain Simulation Review Plan&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Modeling</strong>: replicate Tang Luo&#39;s good-policy list, marking each policy&#39;s &quot;institutionalization degree&quot;</li><li><strong>Variables</strong>: Group A rule-of-man good policies; Group B constitutional good policies</li><li><strong>Time acceleration</strong>: simulate five generations after Tang Luo&#39;s death in the Origin Acceleration Domain</li><li><strong>Review expectation</strong>: Group A good-policy retention under 20%, Group B retention near 100% — quantifying &quot;institutional half-life&quot;</li></ol><h2 id="vi-setting-self-consistency-retcon-ending-risk-assessment" tabindex="-1">VI. Setting Self-Consistency / Retcon-Ending Risk Assessment <a class="header-anchor" href="#vi-setting-self-consistency-retcon-ending-risk-assessment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;VI. Setting Self-Consistency / Retcon-Ending Risk Assessment&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Setting self-consistency: medium-high</strong>. &quot;Sage lord in office, all under heaven well-governed&quot; fits traditional narrative logic</li><li><strong>Retcon-ending risk: medium</strong>. Typical ending path: the author late finds &quot;as soon as Tang Luo dies it restores&quot; is hard to write, so forcibly makes Tang Luo immortal, reincarnates, forever present — this uses setting cheats to solve institutional absence, extremely retcon-tasting</li><li><strong>Protagonist uniqueness dependency</strong>: high (bestowal-style). Pseudo-universal-benefit and bestowal-style are two sides of the same coin; this entry is their theoretical summary</li></ul><p><strong>Old-new cosmos resource comparison</strong>: old-cosmos resources and Great Dao are finite, inevitably predatory involution; Anchor Symbiosis has everyone inherently possessing infinite origin, predation loses meaning.</p><h2 id="five-core-takeaways" tabindex="-1">Five Core Takeaways <a class="header-anchor" href="#five-core-takeaways" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Five Core Takeaways&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Anti-energy waste</strong>: good policies have extremely short half-life due to no institutions; civilization investment is depreciated</li><li><strong>Anti-predation</strong>: not writing good policies into rights is equivalent to allowing withdrawal at any time</li><li><strong>Tang-Luo upper-limit comparison</strong>: Tang Luo&#39;s good is real, but real good also needs a constitution to guard</li><li><strong>Myriad Races full coverage</strong>: institutions cover 99%; good will can only cover those seen</li><li><strong>Hyper-multiverse full openness</strong>: cross-plane order can only rely on laws, not on sages</li><li><strong>Universal equal infinite law</strong>: basic infinite power is inherent in all; the easygoing retain a guarantee, strivers can superimpose higher; infinite origin erases predatory motivation, predatory involution loses meaning from the root</li></ul><h2 id="conclusion" tabindex="-1">Conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Tang Luo&#39;s universal benefit is called &quot;pseudo&quot; not because he did too little, but because what he did did not become &quot;law.&quot; Heng Yuan Zhou&#39;s judgment: good will is worthy of respect, but good will cannot substitute for legislation. If a civilization pins hope on the next Tang Luo, it will always be waiting for a savior — and saviors never come the second time.</p><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Related documents: <a href="./03-共生文明宪章.html">03-Symbiotic Civilization Charter</a> · <a href="./40-共生宪法总纲.html">40-Symbiotic Constitution General Outline</a> · <a href="./01-锚点共生文明宣言.html">01-Anchor Symbiotic Civilization Manifesto</a></li></ul>',28)])])}const m=i(a,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
